+ *    DCS-DMA mutual exclusion, a runtime write during composition corrupts
+ *    the DSC stream (full-screen noise banded at slice boundaries; a bad HS
+ *    misframe can even make the DDIC execute a phantom command and go dark)
+ *    until a full panel re-init. One-way "wait for frame done" polling is
+ *    NOT sufficient, and neither is downstream's 0x1b8[16] cmd-burst bit on
+ *    this mainline programming of the controller.
+ *
+ * 2. 0x53 must stay 0x00 (see lg_panel_enable()): with BCTRL set, 0x51
+ *    latches but is never applied.
+ */
+#define LG_SW43402_BRIGHTNESS	(LG_SW43402_BRIGHTNESS_MAX * 40 / 100)
+
+/*
+ * Experiment matrix for the slider-drag corruption (2026-07-27). The interlock
+ * in dsi_host.c measurably works -- every drag write waits for frame-done and
+ * fires with DSI_STATUS0 == 0 -- and the panel corrupts anyway. So the trigger
+ * is not a mid-frame landing; it is something about the write itself that the
+ * vendor stack does differently. Three candidates, one param each, one build:
+ *
+ * bl_mode: what update_status() actually transmits.
+ *   0 = 0x51 <level>, the real thing (default)
+ *   1 = DCS NOP (0x00) -- same LP excursion on the wire, no brightness
+ *       semantic. Corruption here means "any packet between frames" breaks
+ *       the DDIC; no corruption means 0x51 itself is the problem.
+ *   2 = 0x53 0x24 -- a harmless real register write, same length as 0x51.
+ *   3 = transmit nothing -- control case, proves the slider redraw alone
+ *       is innocent.
+ *
+ * bl_hs: send the write in HS instead of LP. The vendor's only runtime
+ *   command while the display is live (lp-mode-on/off, 53 08/00) is sent
